## Fetch User Function

Here's a clean JavaScript function that fetches a user by ID and returns their name:

```javascript
async function fetchUserName(userId) {
  if (!userId) {
    throw new Error("User ID is required");
  }

  const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`);

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`Failed to fetch user: ${response.status} ${response.statusText}`);
  }

  const user = await response.json();

  if (!user.name) {
    throw new Error("User name not found in response");
  }

  return user.name;
}
```

### Usage Example

```javascript
// Basic usage
try {
  const name = await fetchUserName(123);
  console.log(name); // "John Doe"
} catch (error) {
  console.error(error.message);
}

// Or with .then()/.catch()
fetchUserName(123)
  .then((name) => console.log(name))
  .catch((error) => console.error(error.message));
```

### With Additional Options (Headers, Timeout)

```javascript
async function fetchUserName(userId, options = {}) {
  if (!userId) {
    throw new Error("User ID is required");
  }

  const controller = new AbortController();
  const timeout = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), options.timeout ?? 5000);

  try {
    const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`, {
      signal: controller.signal,
      headers: {
        "Content-Type": "application/json",
        ...options.headers,
      },
    });

    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`Failed to fetch user: ${response.status} ${response.statusText}`);
    }

    const user = await response.json();

    if (!user.name) {
      throw new Error("User name not found in response");
    }

    return user.name;
  } catch (error) {
    if (error.name === "AbortError") {
      throw new Error("Request timed out");
    }
    throw error;
  } finally {
    clearTimeout(timeout);
  }
}
```

### Usage with Options

```javascript
const name = await fetchUserName(123, {
  timeout: 3000,
  headers: { Authorization: "Bearer token123" },
});
```

## Key Considerations

| Feature | Description |
|---|---|
| `response.ok` check | Catches 4xx/5xx HTTP errors |
| `AbortController` | Prevents hanging requests |
| Input validation | Guards against missing/invalid IDs |
| Error propagation | Throws meaningful errors for the caller to handle |

> **Note:** The function assumes the API returns a JSON object with a `name` property (e.g., `{ id: 1, name: "John Doe" }`). Adjust `user.name` to match your actual API response shape.

