## Fetching URL Response Statuses

Here's a clean implementation that fetches response statuses for a list of URLs while preserving order:

```javascript
/**
 * Fetches response statuses for a list of URLs in order.
 * @param {string[]} urls - Array of URLs to fetch
 * @param {Object} options - Optional configuration
 * @param {number} options.timeout - Request timeout in milliseconds (default: 5000)
 * @param {boolean} options.parallel - Fetch in parallel or sequentially (default: true)
 * @returns {Promise<Array<{url: string, status: number|null, statusText: string, error: string|null}>>}
 */
async function fetchResponseStatuses(urls, options = {}) {
  const { timeout = 5000, parallel = true } = options;

  if (!Array.isArray(urls) || urls.length === 0) {
    throw new Error("URLs must be a non-empty array");
  }

  const fetchStatus = async (url) => {
    const controller = new AbortController();
    const timeoutId = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), timeout);

    try {
      const response = await fetch(url, {
        method: "HEAD", // Use HEAD to avoid downloading the body
        signal: controller.signal,
      });

      return {
        url,
        status: response.status,
        statusText: response.statusText,
        error: null,
      };
    } catch (error) {
      return {
        url,
        status: null,
        statusText: null,
        error: error.name === "AbortError" ? "Request timed out" : error.message,
      };
    } finally {
      clearTimeout(timeoutId);
    }
  };

  if (parallel) {
    // Fetch all URLs concurrently, results maintain input order via Promise.all
    return Promise.all(urls.map(fetchStatus));
  } else {
    // Fetch URLs one at a time sequentially
    const results = [];
    for (const url of urls) {
      results.push(await fetchStatus(url));
    }
    return results;
  }
}
```

---

### Usage Examples

```javascript
// Basic usage
const urls = [
  "https://example.com",
  "https://httpstat.us/404",
  "https://httpstat.us/500",
  "https://nonexistent.invalid",
];

// Parallel fetching (faster)
const statuses = await fetchResponseStatuses(urls);
console.log(statuses);
/*
[
  { url: 'https://example.com',        status: 200, statusText: 'OK',                    error: null },
  { url: 'https://httpstat.us/404',    status: 404, statusText: 'Not Found',             error: null },
  { url: 'https://httpstat.us/500',    status: 500, statusText: 'Internal Server Error', error: null },
  { url: 'https://nonexistent.invalid',status: null,statusText: null,                    error: 'Failed to fetch' }
]
*/

// Sequential fetching with custom timeout
const sequentialStatuses = await fetchResponseStatuses(urls, {
  timeout: 3000,
  parallel: false,
});

// Filter only successful responses
const successful = statuses.filter((r) => r.status >= 200 && r.status < 300);

// Filter failed requests
const failed = statuses.filter((r) => r.error !== null);
```

---

### Key Design Decisions

| Feature | Details |
|---|---|
| **Order preserved** | `Promise.all` maintains input array order regardless of response time |
| **HEAD requests** | Avoids downloading response bodies, faster and lighter |
| **Timeout support** | Uses `AbortController` to cancel hung requests |
| **Error handling** | Network errors return `null` status with an error message instead of throwing |
| **Parallel vs Sequential** | Parallel is faster; sequential is useful for rate-limited APIs |

> **Note:** Some servers don't support `HEAD` requests and may return `405 Method Not Allowed`. In that case, switch `"HEAD"` to `"GET"` in the fetch call.

